g('dangerous-goods-classes-explained', 'dg', 'Dangerous goods classes|the nine classes explained',
  'The nine classes of dangerous goods, with examples, packing groups and the labels and documents shippers need.',
  'Dangerous goods are sorted into nine classes by the main hazard. The class decides the packing, the labels and whether a carrier accepts it.',
  [('The nine classes', [('table', ['Class', 'Hazard', 'Examples'], [['1', 'Explosives', 'Fireworks, ammunition'], ['2', 'Gases', 'LPG, aerosols, oxygen'], ['3', 'Flammable liquids', 'Paint, petrol, solvents'], ['4', 'Flammable solids', 'Matches, sulphur'], ['5', 'Oxidisers and organic peroxides', 'Bleaching agents'], ['6', 'Toxic and infectious', 'Pesticides'], ['7', 'Radioactive', 'Medical isotopes'], ['8', 'Corrosives', 'Acids, batteries'], ['9', 'Miscellaneous', 'Lithium batteries, dry ice']])]),
   ('Packing groups', 'Many classes have packing groups I, II and III, for high, medium and low danger. The group sets the packaging strength.'),
   ('What a shipper must do', ['Classify the product using the safety data sheet.', 'Use UN approved packaging and mark and label it.', 'Prepare a dangerous goods declaration.', 'Tell the carrier at booking, because many do not accept some classes.']),
   ('Penalties', 'Wrong declaration is a safety risk and can lead to fines, cargo seizure and a ban from carriers. Do not hide the hazard.')],
  [('Is my product dangerous?', 'Check the SDS section 14 for the UN number and the class. Our DG checker helps.'),
   ('Where do I find labels?', 'Our DG labels tool makes the standard labels.'),
   ('Can I ship by courier?', 'Only if the courier accepts it, and in limited quantities.')],
  ('dg-cargo-checker', 'Open the DG cargo checker'))
g('imdg-vs-iata-dgr-dangerous-goods', 'dg', 'IMDG vs IATA DGR|sea and air dangerous goods rules',
  'The difference between the IMDG Code for sea and the IATA Dangerous Goods Regulations for air, and which applies to your shipment.',
  'Sea and air have their own dangerous goods rules. They are similar, but not the same. Quantities, packing and documents differ.',
  [('IMDG Code', 'The International Maritime Dangerous Goods Code, from the IMO. It covers sea transport, including the segregation of cargo on the ship.'),
   ('IATA DGR', 'The Dangerous Goods Regulations from IATA, based on the ICAO technical instructions. Air has tighter quantity limits per package and some items are forbidden on passenger aircraft.'),
   ('Main differences', [('table', ['Point', 'Sea (IMDG)', 'Air (IATA DGR)'], [['Quantity limits', 'Larger', 'Much smaller per package'], ['Segregation', 'On the ship', 'On the aircraft'], ['Cargo aircraft only', 'Not used', 'Marks some items'], ['Training', 'Required', 'Required']])]),
   ('Road and rail', 'Road and rail inside a country follow national rules. Many countries follow ADR in Europe or similar.')],
  [('Do I need training?', 'Yes. People who pack, mark and sign the declaration need dangerous goods training for the mode.'),
   ('Can one declaration cover both?', 'No. Use the one for the mode, and the form the carrier asks for.'),
   ('How often do the rules change?', 'Every year for air and every two years for sea, with a transition period.')],
  ('dg-cargo-checker', 'Open the DG cargo checker'))
g('un-numbers-and-proper-shipping-names', 'dg', 'UN numbers and proper shipping names|how to read them',
  'What a UN number and a proper shipping name are, how to find them on the safety data sheet and why they must match on every document.',
  'Every regulated dangerous substance has a four digit UN number and a standard name. They go on the packs and on the declaration.',
  [('UN number', 'Four digits, such as UN1993 for a flammable liquid not otherwise specified. It identifies the substance or group in the rules.'),
   ('Proper shipping name', 'The official name used in the rules. A technical name may be added for generic entries.'),
   ('Where to find them', 'In section 14 of the safety data sheet, and in the IMDG and IATA lists.'),
   ('Match everywhere', 'The UN number, name, class and packing group must be the same on the pack, the declaration, the booking and the BL or AWB.')],
  [('Does every chemical have a UN number?', 'No. Only those that meet the hazard criteria.'),
   ('What if the SDS is wrong?', 'Do not rely on it. Check with a DG specialist.'),
   ('What is N.O.S.?', 'Not otherwise specified. A generic name for products not listed by name.')],
  ('dg-cargo-checker', 'Open the DG cargo checker'))
g('safety-data-sheet-sds-explained', 'dg', 'Safety data sheet (SDS)|the 16 sections',
  'What a safety data sheet (SDS or MSDS) is, its 16 sections and which ones a shipper and a forwarder use for transport.',
  'The SDS is the supplier document that describes a chemical, its hazards and how to handle it. A forwarder needs it to book dangerous or doubtful cargo.',
  [('The 16 sections', 'They run from identification and hazards, through composition, first aid, firefighting, handling, exposure controls and properties, to transport, regulation and other information.'),
   ('What matters for transport', ['Section 2: hazards and classification.', 'Section 9: flash point and other properties.', 'Section 14: UN number, class, packing group and marine pollutant status.']),
   ('Use it right', 'Ask for a current SDS from the manufacturer, in English. An out of date one can give the wrong class.'),
   ('Not a transport document', 'The SDS is not a dangerous goods declaration. The shipper must still prepare one that follows the mode rules.')],
  [('Is an MSDS the same as an SDS?', 'Yes. MSDS is the older name.'),
   ('Who writes it?', 'The manufacturer or the supplier.'),
   ('Do all products need an SDS for shipping?', 'Carriers ask for one for chemicals and mixed goods, even when they are not dangerous, to confirm.')],
  ('dg-cargo-checker', 'Open the DG cargo checker'))
g('lithium-battery-shipping-guide', 'dg', 'Shipping lithium batteries|UN3480, UN3481, UN3090 and UN3091',
  'How to ship lithium batteries by air and sea: UN numbers, test summary, state of charge, packing and why many carriers refuse them.',
  'Lithium batteries are dangerous goods of class 9. Fires in transit have made carriers and regulators strict.',
  [('The four UN numbers', [('table', ['UN number', 'What it is'], [['UN3480', 'Lithium ion batteries on their own'], ['UN3481', 'Lithium ion batteries packed with or in equipment'], ['UN3090', 'Lithium metal batteries on their own'], ['UN3091', 'Lithium metal batteries packed with or in equipment']])]),
   ('Key rules', ['Cells and batteries must pass the UN 38.3 tests, and a test summary is needed.', 'Stand-alone lithium ion batteries (UN3480) are for cargo aircraft only, and the state of charge is limited to 30 percent.', 'Packing, marking and the class 9 lithium battery label depend on the size and watt hours.']),
   ('Damaged or recalled batteries', 'Are forbidden or need special approval. Do not ship them.'),
   ('Booking', 'Tell your forwarder at the time of booking. Many airlines and lines have their own extra limits.')],
  [('Can I ship a phone by courier?', 'A phone with its battery fitted can be shipped under UN3481 conditions, if the courier accepts it.'),
   ('How do I know the watt hours?', 'It is printed on the battery, or calculate volts times amp-hours.'),
   ('Is there a checker?', 'Yes. Our lithium battery checker helps you with the class and the packing.')],
  ('lithium-battery-checker', 'Open the lithium battery checker'))
g('wood-packaging-ispm-15', 'dg', 'ISPM 15 wood packaging|the stamp that most countries ask for',
  'What ISPM 15 is, which wood packaging needs the stamp, the treatments allowed and what happens to untreated pallets and crates.',
  'To stop pests from travelling in wood, most countries ask that wood packaging is treated and stamped under the international standard ISPM 15.',
  [('What is covered', 'Pallets, crates, boxes, dunnage and cable drums made from raw wood. Plywood, particle board and thin wood under about 6 mm are generally exempt.'),
   ('Treatment and stamp', 'The wood is heat treated (HT) or, where still allowed, fumigated. It is marked with the IPPC symbol, a country code, the producer code and the treatment code.'),
   ('If it is not compliant', 'The consignment can be refused, fumigated at your cost or destroyed. Australia, the US, the EU and China check closely.'),
   ('Tips', ['Buy pallets from a registered treater.', 'Check the stamp is clear, on two sides.', 'For export from India, use pallets treated by an approved agency.'])],
  [('Do plastic and metal pallets need it?', 'No.'),
   ('Is a certificate needed too?', 'Usually the stamp is enough. Some countries also ask for a fumigation certificate.'),
   ('Can I reuse a pallet?', 'If it is intact, and the stamp is clear and the wood is clean.')],
  ('document-checker', 'Open the document checker'))
g('fumigation-certificate-for-exports', 'dg', 'Fumigation certificates|when exports need one',
  'When an export needs a fumigation certificate, what it covers, who issues it and how to avoid delays at the port.',
  'Some countries ask that cargo or the container is fumigated against pests before shipping. The certificate is the proof.',
  [('When it is needed', 'For some agricultural goods, wood products and the packing, and for some destinations such as Australia and New Zealand. The importer permit or the rules of the destination tell you.'),
   ('How it works', 'A licensed pest control agency treats the cargo or the container with a fumigant, in a set dose and time, then issues the certificate. The container may carry a fumigation warning mark.'),
   ('Safety', 'After fumigation the container must be aired before opening. Staff must follow safety rules, and the carrier must know that the container was fumigated.'),
   ('Timing', 'Book the agency early. The treatment, the airing and the certificate take days, and they fit into the cut-off.')],
  [('Is a fumigation certificate the same as a phytosanitary one?', 'No. The phytosanitary certificate is from the plant protection authority. The fumigation one is from the treatment agency.'),
   ('Who pays?', 'The shipper, unless the terms say otherwise.'),
   ('Can I fumigate in the container?', 'Yes, when the rules allow and the container is suitable.')],
  ('document-checker', 'Open the document checker'))
g('project-cargo-and-break-bulk-guide', 'dg', 'Project cargo and break bulk|how to plan heavy and oversize moves',
  'How project cargo works: route survey, break bulk and RoRo options, lifting and lashing, permits and the timeline for heavy and oversize equipment.',
  'Project cargo is large, heavy or unusual equipment, such as a boiler, a turbine or a plant. It is planned like a project, not booked like a box.',
  [('Early steps', ['Get exact dimensions, weights, the centre of gravity and the lifting points from the maker.', 'Choose the mode: break bulk ship, RoRo, flat rack, or a charter.', 'Survey the route from the factory to the port, including bridges, roads and cranes.']),
   ('Execution', ['Plan the lifts, lashing and securing with a marine surveyor if needed.', 'Get permits for over-dimensional cargo on road.', 'Insure with a project cargo policy.']),
   ('Documents', 'The same as normal cargo, plus the lifting plan, the stowage plan and the survey reports. Customs may ask for the maker data.'),
   ('Time', 'Start planning months ahead for big items. Ports, cranes and permits are the long poles.')],
  [('What is break bulk?', 'Cargo loaded piece by piece on a ship, not in containers.'),
   ('What is RoRo?', 'Roll on, roll off. Wheeled and tracked cargo drives onto the ship.'),
   ('Can YAM handle it?', 'Yes. See our project cargo service.')],
  ('project-cargo', 'See our project cargo service'))
g('pharma-cargo-shipping-guide', 'dg', 'Shipping pharmaceuticals|cold chain, GDP and paperwork',
  'How to ship pharmaceutical cargo: temperature ranges, good distribution practice, validated packaging, documents and customs for medicines.',
  'Medicines are valuable, regulated and often temperature sensitive. A weak link in the chain can spoil the whole shipment.',
  [('Temperature ranges', [('table', ['Range', 'Typical use'], [['2 to 8 °C', 'Vaccines, biologics, insulin'], ['15 to 25 °C', 'Controlled room temperature'], ['Frozen', 'Some vaccines and samples']])]),
   ('Good distribution practice', 'Use validated packaging or reefers, qualified carriers, temperature data loggers and written procedures. Keep records for audits.'),
   ('Documents', 'Commercial invoice, packing list, the licence and approvals for the product, certificate of analysis and the importer permit of the destination. Controlled substances need extra permits.'),
   ('Customs and delays', 'Pharma clearance is stricter at many ports. Pre-clear the documents and have a plan for storage at the airport.')],
  [('Can I ship pharma by courier?', 'Small lots, samples and some products, with the right permits.'),
   ('What if the logger shows an excursion?', 'Tell the consignee and the quality team at once. They decide whether to use or reject.'),
   ('Where can I read more?', 'See our pharmaceuticals service page.')],
  ('pharmaceuticals', 'See our pharma logistics'))
